**Allowed characters:** The page doesn't list a character set. It says the values must be ones that can be sent in the `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` HTTP header, such as registered names from the IANA WebSocket Subprotocol Name Registry (`soap`, `wamp`, `ship`) or custom names that the client and server both understand. Character rules come from the WebSocket Protocol spec. The constructor throws a `SyntaxError` if a value appears more than once or doesn't meet those requirements.

**If the server doesn't select one:** The page says:

> The connection is not established until the sub-protocol is negotiated with the server. The selected protocol can then be read from `WebSocket.protocol`: it will be the empty string if a connection cannot be established.

The page doesn't say what happens when the handshake succeeds but the server picks none of the offered protocols. Check `socket.protocol` in the `open` handler to see which protocol was negotiated.

```js
const socket = new WebSocket("wss://example.org/chat", ["chat.v2", "chat.v1"]);

socket.addEventListener("open", () => {
  console.log("Negotiated protocol:", socket.protocol); // "" if none was selected
});
```

**Can headers be set?** No, not through this constructor. It accepts only `url` and `protocols`, and the page doesn't mention a way to add custom HTTP headers. The `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header is set only through the `protocols` argument. If you need to pass credentials, common approaches (not covered on this page) include putting a token in the URL query string or using a cookie, which the browser sends automatically for the same site.

